<BaseLayout title="How to Read Food Intolerance Test Results - FoodCheck" description="Learn how to interpret food intolerance test reports — IgG antibody levels, hydrogen breath test results, and what the numbers actually mean for your diet." author="Dr. Emily Carter, Registered Dietitian" datePublished="2026-03-01" dateModified="2026-07-20" breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { label: 'Reading Test Results', href: '/guides/reading-test-results' }]}" medical={true}>
<Header /><main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 md:py-24">
<h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-6 tracking-tight">Reading Food Intolerance Test Results</h1>
<p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mb-4">Got test results and not sure what they mean? Here is a guide to interpreting common food intolerance test reports.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-8">By Dr. Emily Carter, Registered Dietitian · Updated 20 Jul 2026</p>
<Image src={resultsImage} alt="Medical report showing food intolerance test results being reviewed" class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl mb-8" width={800} height={450} loading="lazy" format="webp" />
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">IgG Test Results</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">IgG tests typically show antibody levels for each food tested, often categorised as low, moderate, or high reactivity. However, many medical authorities do not recommend IgG testing because IgG antibodies may simply indicate exposure to a food, not intolerance.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">Hydrogen Breath Test</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">A rise in breath hydrogen of 20 ppm or more above baseline after consuming a test sugar (lactose, fructose, lactulose) is typically considered positive for malabsorption.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">Elimination Diet Results</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Clear symptom improvement during elimination followed by recurrence on reintroduction of a specific food is the most reliable evidence of intolerance. Document everything in a food diary.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">When to See a Doctor</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600">Always discuss test results with your GP or a registered dietitian before making major dietary changes. Never self-diagnose based on commercial test results alone.</p>
</main><Footer /></BaseLayout>
